- helm
- net
- pass
- mesa_foot
- desk

' This module is the packet pump of the foot at /io/mesa. It has the actors
' that own the socket and the clock, and the loop that runs the rules of the
' foot. The pump does these things:
' - It reads the bytes of each connection and gives the packets to the rules.
' - It sends the packets that the rules ask it to send.
' - It tells the rules the time, one tick after the other.
' - It asks its node to do what the rules ask, one errand after the other
'   (mesa_wire/wish).
' - It keeps the secret files of the foot, and it signs when the desk asks
'   (desk.foil).
'
' No code here makes a decision. The rules are in mesa_foot, net and pass.
'
' The record of the foot, /<self>/io/mesa, tells where the foot listens and
' where its two files are:
' - `port`
' - `ring` (the seed that the world signs with, 32 bytes)
' - `mark` (its rift and the highest epoch that it announced)
'
' The two files are intentionally outside the world. The seed is not a record.
' The mark must stay through a restore of the world, to show that a restore
' occurred. The card of each peer, /<self>/io/mesa/<peer>, tells its `key`,
' its `host` and its `port`.
'
' Bytes go between worlds in short connections. One connection is the number
' of the sender in 16 bytes, then each packet after its length in 4 bytes. A
' connection gives the number of its sender, but the foot does not believe
' this number. The foot uses the number only to find where an answer goes. It
' sends an answer to the address that this world holds for that peer, and not
' back through the connection. Each page has a signature.
'
' The foot has two more files: `custody` and `maker` (desk.foil). The foot
' makes them the first time that its desk must have them. What a door gets for
' the foot, the secret of an invitation, is a file in io/mesa/in. The name of
' this file is a number. What the foot leaves for the door, the secret of an
' invitation that it minted, is a file in io/mesa/out.
'
' The desk also answers while the foot is silent. In this way, a world that
' was prepared under a key that is not its own becomes its own by a claim. If
' a world keeps no grant for its door, the foot gives the world its first
' session there. The foot leaves the secret in io/mesa/out/session for the
' person who is at the machine.
'
' The foot stops when its record is removed or holds `stop`. It stops its
' clock and its acceptors, closes the socket that it listens on, and then
' stops.
'
' When a world's own card holds an identity log, the world signs with the key
' that the log states. When the log moves to a new key, the foot reads its
' ring again. While the key of the ring and the key of the log do not agree,
' the foot sends no packet and hears no packet.
+ mesa_wire
  + heard
    ' These are the packets of one connection and the number that their sender
    ' gave.
    : from=nat packets=row[nat]
  + tick
    : now=nat
  + task
    & feet/task/ack
      feet/task/fact
      feet/task/binary
      mesa_wire/heard
      mesa_wire/tick
      mesa_wire/wish
  + wish
    ' This is an errand that a part of the foot has for the world
    ' (mesa_foot/errand). The foot sends it to its own mailbox. Thus each part
    ' of the foot can ask, and the loop of the foot does one errand at a
    ' time.
    '
    ' The foot has one way to write: it pokes the errand on its node,
    ' /<self>/io/mesa. The hand of the node does what the errand says and
    ' makes the writes in the event of that poke (mesa_foot/talk). The answer
    ' to the poke is the answer of the kernel to that event. The foot sends
    ' no other poke until that answer comes. Thus each answer is the answer
    ' to the first wish that waits.
    : request=nat errand=mesa_foot/errand
  + whole
    ' This is the read that gives the world as it is now. A committed world
    ' is in the store. Thus the transfer of the world copies no data.
    \ state=namespace
    ^ [nat any nat]
    [200 state 0]
  + world
    ' This function gives the answer of mesa_wire/whole its type.
    \ body=any
    ^ namespace
    body
  + look
    ' This function asks the supervisor for the world as it is now.
    \ parent=nat
    ^ nat
    | Send parent (feet/gift/snapshot_bat 0 mesa_wire/whole)
  + ask
    ' This function pokes the first errand that waits, if there is one.
    \ parent=nat queue=row[mesa_wire/wish]
    ^ nat
    ? !(queue.len) 0
    | Send parent (feet/gift/pass mesa_wire/here (writ/poke (myth/sing ['errand] queue.at(0).errand)))
  + note
    ' This function gives the errand that pokes a record on the node of the
    ' foot.
    \ home=path word=myth
    ^ mesa_foot/errand
    = writes=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[home (writ/poke word)]]
    | mesa_foot/bid (mesa_foot/order/write writes)
  + frames
    ' This function gives the bytes of one connection: the 16 bytes of the
    ' sender, and then each packet after the 4 bytes of its length. It makes a
    ' byte tree of these parts (bat_concat), and the runtime flattens the tree
    ' in one pass.
    \ me=nat packets=row[nat]
    ^ nat
    = head=row[any] [(bat_bytes (mod me (bex 128)) 16)]
      parts
        | packets.fold head
          \ out=row[any] packet=nat
          ^ row[any]
          = len (dec (wid packet))
          | out.snoc((bat_bytes len 4)).snoc((bat_bar packet))
    | bat_flatten (bat_concat parts.snoc((bat_bytes 1 1)))
  + unframe
    ' This function gives the packets in the bytes of one connection. It gives
    ' none when the bytes are not frames.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[mesa_wire/heard]
    = none=maybe[mesa_wire/heard] .none
      empty=row[nat] []
      size (dec (wid bytes))
    ? (lt size 16) none
    = from (loadvar 0 16 bytes)
    % at=nat 16
      out=row[nat] empty
    ^ maybe[mesa_wire/heard]
    ? (eq at size) (./some (mesa_wire/heard from out))
    ? (gt (add at 4) size) none
    = len (loadvar at 4 bytes)
      end (add (add at 4) len)
    ? (gt end size) none
    | _ end out.snoc((add (loadvar (add at 4) len bytes) (bex (mul 8 len))))
  + acceptor
    ' The acceptor waits on the shared socket that the foot listens on. It
    ' reads one connection to its end and tells the foot what the connection
    ' contained. It waits again when the foot tells it to continue. The answer
    ' of the foot is what stops the acceptor. When a foot stops, it connects
    ' to itself to wake each acceptor, and it answers no.
    \ fd=nat self=nat
    ^ nat
    = ;[hello caps] (Recv[nat] 0)
      foot caps.at(0)
      nothing=row[nat] []
      none=maybe[mesa_wire/heard] .none
    % ignored=nat 0
    = ;connection (Accept fd)
      ;bytes (ReadAll connection mesa_foot/most_read)
      got=maybe[mesa_wire/heard] (? (eq 0 bytes) none (mesa_wire/unframe bytes))
      carried got.fall((mesa_wire/heard 0 nothing))
    ; (CloseFd connection)
    ; (SendCaps foot carried [0])
    = ;[go more] (Recv[nat] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps more)
    ? !(go) 0
    | _ 0
  + sender
    ' The sender moves what the foot sends to one peer. Each batch is one
    ' connection. If the sender cannot reach a peer, the batch is lost. The
    ' engine asks again and hints repeat. Thus no batch is put in a queue.
    \ host=str port=nat me=nat self=nat
    ^ nat
    % ignored=nat 0
    = ;[batch caps] (Recv[row[nat]] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
    ; (SendTo 0 host port (mesa_wire/frames me batch))
    | _ 0
  + ticker
    ' The ticker tells the foot the time each second, for as long as the foot
    ' tells it to continue.
    \ self=nat
    ^ nat
    = ;[hello caps] (Recv[nat] 0)
      foot caps.at(0)
    % ignored=nat 0
    ; (Sleep 1)
    ; (SendCaps foot (mesa_wire/tick (div (Now 0) 1000000)) [0])
    = ;[go more] (Recv[nat] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps more)
    ? !(go) 0
    | _ 0
  + route
    ' This is a peer as the foot's binding gives it, with the address where
    ' the peer is reached.
    : peer=mesa_foot/peer host=str port=nat
  + routes
    ' This function gives the peers that a binding names: each child that
    ' holds a key and an address.
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ row[mesa_wire/route]
    = none=row[mesa_wire/route] []
    | binding.kid_tap.fold none
      \ out=row[mesa_wire/route] [key tree]
      ^ row[mesa_wire/route]
      ?> (./some version) tree.fil out
      = card (unpin version.myth)
        held card.nat_at(['key] 0)
        port card.nat_at(['port] 0)
      ? (eq 0 held) out
      ? (eq 0 port) out
      | out.snoc((mesa_wire/route (mesa_foot/peer key (quip_val key) held) card.txt_at(['host]) port))
  + own
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ myth
    ?> (./some version) binding.fil myth/new
    | unpin version.myth
  + link
    ' This is a sender for one peer, and the handle that the foot uses to
    ' reach the sender.
    : id=nat host=str port=nat handle=nat
  + wire
    ' This function gives senders for the peers that a binding names. It keeps
    ' a sender if the address of its peer did not change.
    \ me=nat wanted=row[mesa_wire/route] had=row[mesa_wire/link]
    ^ row[mesa_wire/link]
    = none=row[mesa_wire/link] []
      ;kept
        | had.fold none
          \ ;out=row[mesa_wire/link] item=mesa_wire/link
          ^ row[mesa_wire/link]
          ? (wanted.any((\ want=mesa_wire/route (and (eq want.peer.id item.id) (and (equal[str] want.host item.host) (eq want.port item.port)))))) out.snoc(item)
          ; (CloseHandle item.handle)
          out
    | wanted.fold kept
      \ ;out=row[mesa_wire/link] want=mesa_wire/route
      ^ row[mesa_wire/link]
      ? (out.any((\ item=mesa_wire/link (eq item.id want.peer.id)))) out
      = ;handle (Spawn (mesa_wire/sender want.host want.port me))
      | out.snoc((mesa_wire/link want.peer.id want.host want.port handle))
  + why
    ' This function gives the reason that the kernel gave when it did not
    ' accept a poke. It gives none when the kernel accepted the poke.
    \ err=maybe[pail]
    ^ maybe[iota]
    = none=maybe[iota] .none
      unknown=iota 'net_refused
    ?> (./some refusal) err none
    ?> (./some said) (pail/as[helm/supervisor/rejection] refusal) (./some unknown)
    | ./some said.reason
  + act
    ' This function does what the rules ask. It gives the packets of each peer
    ' to the sender of that peer as one batch. It puts each errand in the
    ' mailbox of the foot (mesa_wire/wish).
    \ links=row[mesa_wire/link] does=row[mesa_foot/effect]
    ^ nat
    = empty=row[nat] []
      ;sent
        | links.fold 0
          \ ;done=nat item=mesa_wire/link
          ^ nat
          = batch
              | does.fold empty
                \ out=row[nat] effect=mesa_foot/effect
                ^ row[nat]
                ?> (./send to packet) effect out
                ? !(eq to item.id) out
                | out.snoc(packet)
          ? !(batch.len) done
          ; (Send item.handle batch)
          done.inc
    | does.fold sent
      \ ;done=nat effect=mesa_foot/effect
      ^ nat
      ?> (./admit request errand) effect done
      ; (Send 0 (mesa_wire/wish request errand))
      done.inc
  + marked
    ' This function gives the rift and the highest announced epoch that a mark
    ' file holds. It gives zeros when there is no mark file at that time.
    \ file=str
    ^ mesa_wire/mark
    = ;bytes (Try ReadFile file)
    ? bytes
     > (./err _) (mesa_wire/mark 0 0)
     > (./ok held)
      ? (lt (wid held) 17) (mesa_wire/mark 0 0)
      | mesa_wire/mark (loadvar 0 8 held) (loadvar 8 8 held)
  + mark
    : rift=nat top=nat
  + remark
    \ file=str rift=nat top=nat
    ^ nat
    = raw (add (mod rift (bex 64)) (lsh (mod top (bex 64)) 64))
    | WriteFile file (add raw (bex 128))
  + named
    ' This function gives the path of a file as the foot's record gives it.
    ' When the record gives no path, the function gives the path where the
    ' file is kept.
    \ said=str kept=str
    ^ str
    ? (eq 0 said.len) kept
    said
  + seed
    ' This function gives the seed that the world signs with, from the ring
    ' file of the world. If a world did not state a key and has no ring, the
    ' function gives it a new seed. The new seed is 32 bytes of the runtime's
    ' entropy. The function writes the seed where the ring is kept, in the
    ' world's own folder. If a world stated a key, the function does not give
    ' it a different seed. Without its ring, that world stays silent, because
    ' a new seed is a new identity.
    \ ring=str stated=bool
    ^ nat
    = ;held (Try ReadFile ring)
      found
        ? held
         > (./err _) 0
         > (./ok bytes) bytes
    ? !(eq 0 (mesa_wire/public found)) found
    ? stated 0
    = ;folder (Folder "io/mesa")
      ;fresh (Entropy 32)
      ;wrote (WriteFile ring fresh)
    fresh
  + took
    ' This function gives the bytes that a file holds, or no bytes.
    \ file=str
    ^ nat
    = ;held (Try ReadFile file)
    ? held
     > (./err _) 0
     > (./ok bytes) bytes
  + address
    ' This function gives the address where a world is reached. The one that
    ' started the world left the address for the foot in a file. The file has
    ' the name of the host and a port, as the one line "host port". The port
    ' is 0 when the file holds no such line. A world gets its address at each
    ' start, as it gets its founding at its first start. Thus, when a world is
    ' started in a different place, the world itself tells where it is.
    \ handed=nat
    ^ [host=str port=nat]
    = nothing=str ""
      none=[host=str port=nat] [nothing 0]
    ? (lt (wid handed) 2) none
    = text (str/from_atom (mod handed (bex (mul 8 (dec (wid handed))))))
      parts (str/split_byte 32 text)
    ? !(eq 2 parts.len) none
    ?> (./some port) (nat/parse parts.at(1)) none
    = got=[host=str port=nat] [parts.at(0) port]
    got
  + secret
    ' This function gives a secret that the foot keeps as a file of its own.
    ' It reads the secret, or it makes the secret from the runtime's entropy
    ' the first time that the secret is necessary. The file is in the folder
    ' that only the world can open.
    \ file=str
    ^ nat
    = ;found (mesa_wire/took file)
    ? (gt (wid found) 32) found
    = ;folder (Folder "io/mesa")
      ;fresh (Entropy 32)
      ;wrote (WriteFile file fresh)
    fresh
  + handed
    ' This function gives the path where a door leaves what it got for the
    ' foot. The path is a file in io/mesa/in, and the name of the file is only
    ' a number. Thus an ask can name no other file of the world.
    \ n=nat
    ^ str
    | str/cat_all ["io/mesa/in/" (nat/show n)]
  + leave
    ' This function leaves the secret of an invitation for the door of this
    ' world. The secret is a file in io/mesa/out, and the name of the file is
    ' the number of the invitation.
    \ k=desk/keys id=nat
    ^ nat
    ? (eq 0 id) 0
    = ;folder (Folder "io/mesa/out")
      ;wrote (WriteFile (str/cat_all ["io/mesa/out/" (nat/show id)]) (desk/invitation k id))
    1
  + opening
    ' This function makes the first session of this world. It makes a secret
    ' here and leaves the secret as text in io/mesa/out/session for the person
    ' who is at the machine. Then it asks the world for the grant for that
    ' secret (desk/first).
    \ links=row[mesa_wire/link] k=desk/keys state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    = ;folder (Folder "io/mesa/out")
      ;fresh (Entropy 32)
      token (nat/show fresh)
      ;wrote (WriteFile "io/mesa/out/session" (add (str/to_atom token) (bex (mul 8 token.len))))
      turn (desk/first k token (div (Now 0) 1000000000) state foot)
    ; (mesa_wire/act links turn.does)
    1
  + born
    ' With this function, a world that was started under a name keeps the
    ' founding that its host left for it (desk/founded).
    \ links=row[mesa_wire/link] given=nat foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    = text (str/from_atom (mod given (bex (mul 8 (dec (wid given))))))
      turn (desk/founded text foot)
    ; (mesa_wire/act links turn.does)
    1
  + desk
    ' This function answers the asks that the world holds for the desk of its
    ' foot (desk.foil). It reads the custody secret and the maker secret only
    ' when an ask waits, and it makes them the first time. When an answered
    ' ask got a secret, the function makes its file empty and marks the ask
    ' spent.
    '
    ' A world that let no person in and holds no log of its own looks for the
    ' file io/mesa/founding. A host leaves this file when it starts a world
    ' under a name that someone else minted. If the file is there, the world
    ' keeps the founding and waits for a claim. If there is no file, the world
    ' is its own and gets its first session.
    \ links=row[mesa_wire/link] live=nat state=namespace foot=mesa_foot/foot
    ^ nat
    = asks (desk/pending foot.self state)
      used (desk/used foot.self state)
      lone (desk/doorless foot.self state)
    ? (and (and (eq 0 asks.len) (eq 0 used.len)) !lone) 0
    = home (pass/kept (mesa_foot/home foot) (net/sees state)).fall(myth/new)
      ;given (? (and lone (eq 0 home.nat_at(['seq] 0))) (mesa_wire/took "io/mesa/founding") 0)
      prepared (gt (wid given) 1)
      ;kept (? prepared (mesa_wire/born links given foot) 0)
      ;custody (? (and (eq 0 asks.len) !lone) 0 (mesa_wire/secret (mesa_wire/named home.txt_at(['custody_file]) "io/mesa/custody")))
      ;maker (? (eq 0 asks.len) 0 (mesa_wire/secret (mesa_wire/named home.txt_at(['maker]) "io/mesa/maker")))
      k (desk/keys live custody maker)
      ;opened (? (and (and lone !prepared) (desk/own k state foot)) (mesa_wire/opening links k state foot) 0)
      ;answered
        | asks.fold 0
          \ ;done=nat item=[at=path ask=myth]
          ^ nat
          = [where ask] item
            n ask.nat_at(['handed] 0)
            ;given (? (eq 0 n) 0 (mesa_wire/took (mesa_wire/handed n)))
            ;left (? (equal[str] ask.txt_at(['want]) "link") (mesa_wire/leave k ask.nat_at(['id] 0)) 0)
            turn (desk/answer k given where ask state foot)
          ; (mesa_wire/act links turn.does)
          done.inc
    | used.fold answered
      \ ;done=nat item=[at=path handed=nat]
      ^ nat
      = [where n] item
        ;folder (Folder "io/mesa/in")
        ;emptied (WriteFile (mesa_wire/handed n) 1)
        gone=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[where (writ/poke (myth/sing ['spent] (pails/n 1)))]]
      ; (mesa_wire/act links (desk/say gone foot).does)
      done.inc
  + run
    ' This function is the foot. It waits for the world as it is now, and
    ' reads its seed and its mark. It increases its rift when the world is
    ' behind what it announced before. Then it runs the rules for all that
    ' arrives.
    '
    ' The foot listens at the address that it gets at its start
    ' (io/mesa/address), and then it writes that address on its record. If it
    ' gets no address, it listens where its record tells it to listen. When a
    ' port appears on a record that had no port, the foot starts to listen on
    ' that port at that time. A foot that listens keeps its port until it
    ' stops.
    \ init=feet/task/init self=nat
    ^ nat
    = record (mesa_wire/own init.ax)
      ;handed (mesa_wire/took "io/mesa/address")
    = [host given] (mesa_wire/address handed)
    = port (? (eq 0 given) record.nat_at(['port] 0) given)
      ring (mesa_wire/named record.txt_at(['ring]) "io/mesa/ring")
      file (mesa_wire/named record.txt_at(['mark]) "io/mesa/mark")
      claimed (gt record.nat_at(['seq] 0) 0)
      ;seed (mesa_wire/seed ring (claimed || (gt record.nat_at(['key] 0) 0)))
      silent (and claimed !(eq record.nat_at(['key] 0) (mesa_wire/public seed)))
    ? (eq 0 (mesa_wire/public seed))
      ; (Trace ["mesa foot: the ring file holds no seed" ring] 0)
      0
    ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
    = ;world
        % ignored=nat 0
        ^ namespace
        = ;[message caps] (Recv[mesa_wire/task] 0)
        ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
        ?> (./binary request status body mime) message (_ 0)
        | mesa_wire/world body
      ;was (mesa_wire/marked file)
      rift (? (lt world.local.top was.top) was.rift.inc was.rift)
      ;noted (mesa_wire/remark file rift (nmax was.top world.local.top))
      me (mesa_foot/id world.self)
      first (mesa_wire/routes init.ax)
      none=row[mesa_wire/link] []
      no_links=row[mesa_wire/link] []
      no_routes=row[mesa_wire/route] []
      ;links (mesa_wire/wire me first none)
      ;clock (Spawn mesa_wire/ticker)
      ;door (? (eq 0 port) 0 (Listen port))
      helpers (? (eq 0 port) 1 (add 1 mesa_foot/acceptors))
    ; (SendCaps clock 0 [0])
    ; (? (eq 0 port) 0 (mesa_wire/listen door mesa_foot/acceptors))
    ; (? claimed 0 (Send 0 (mesa_wire/wish 0 (mesa_wire/note [world.self 'io 'mesa] (myth/sing ['key] (pails/n (Ed25519PublicKey seed)))))))
    ; (? (eq 0 given) 0 (Send 0 (mesa_wire/wish 0 (mesa_wire/note [world.self 'io 'mesa] (myth/sing ['port] (pails/n given)).put(['host] (pails/t host))))))
    ; (Trace ["mesa foot listening" port] 0)
    = begun (mesa_foot/start world.self seed rift first.map((\ item=mesa_wire/route item.peer)))
      ;opening (mesa_foot/tick (div (Now 0) 1000000) world begun)
    ; (mesa_wire/act links opening.does)
    ; (mesa_wire/desk links seed world opening.foot)
    % foot=mesa_foot/foot opening.foot
      view=namespace world
      reach=row[mesa_wire/link] links
      top=nat (nmax was.top world.local.top)
      beat=nat 0
      left=nat 0
      key=nat seed
      mute=bool silent
      at=nat port
      ear=nat door
      queue=row[mesa_wire/wish] []
    = ;[message caps] (Recv[mesa_wire/task] 0)
      helper
        ? message
         > carried=mesa_wire/heard .true
         > time=mesa_wire/tick .true
        .false
    ; (? (and helper (gt caps.len 0)) (Send caps.at(0) (? left 0 1)) 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
    ? (and helper (eq left 1)) (? (eq 0 ear) 0 (CloseFd ear))
    ? (and helper (gt left 1)) (_ foot view reach top beat left.dec key mute at ear queue)
    ? (gt left 0) (_ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue)
    ? (and mute helper) (_ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue)
    ? message
     > wanted=mesa_wire/wish
      ; (? (queue.len) 0 (mesa_wire/ask init.parent [wanted]))
      | _ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue.snoc(wanted)
     > (./binary request status body mime)
      = held (mesa_wire/world body)
      ? (eq held.local.top view.local.top) (_ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue)
      = ;turn (mesa_foot/turned held foot)
        ;higher (nmax top held.local.top)
      ; (? (gt higher top) (mesa_wire/remark file foot.rift higher) 0)
      ; (mesa_wire/act reach turn.does)
      ; (mesa_wire/desk reach key held turn.foot)
      | _ turn.foot held reach higher beat left key mute at ear queue
     > (./fact binding)
      ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
      = wish (mesa_wire/own binding).nat_at(['port] 0)
        opens (and (eq 0 at) (gt wish 0))
        ;heard (? opens (Listen wish) ear)
        now (? opens wish at)
      ; (? opens (mesa_wire/listen heard mesa_foot/acceptors) 0)
      ; (? opens (Trace ["mesa foot listening" wish] 0) 0)
      ? (mesa_wire/stopped binding)
        ; (mesa_wire/wake now mesa_foot/acceptors)
        ; (mesa_wire/wire me no_routes reach)
        | _ foot view no_links top beat (? (eq 0 now) 1 (add 1 mesa_foot/acceptors)) key mute now heard queue
      = wanted (mesa_wire/routes binding)
        peers wanted.map((\ item=mesa_wire/route item.peer))
        ;next (mesa_wire/wire me wanted reach)
        own (mesa_wire/own binding)
        stated (gt own.nat_at(['seq] 0) 0)
        want own.nat_at(['key] 0)
        ;fresh (? (and stated !(eq want (mesa_wire/public key))) (ReadFile ring) key)
        agrees (? stated (eq want (mesa_wire/public fresh)) .true)
      ? !agrees (_ foot view next top beat left key .true now heard queue)
      ? (and (eq fresh key) (and !mute (equal[row[mesa_foot/peer]] peers foot.peers))) (_ foot view next top beat left key mute now heard queue)
      = ;again (mesa_foot/tick (div (Now 0) 1000000) view (mesa_foot/start view.self fresh foot.rift peers))
      ; (mesa_wire/act next again.does)
      | _ again.foot view next top beat left fresh .false now heard queue
     > carried=mesa_wire/heard
      = ;turn
          | carried.packets.fold (mesa_foot/quiet foot)
            \ ;acc=mesa_foot/turn packet=nat
            ^ mesa_foot/turn
            | mesa_foot/after acc
              \ now=mesa_foot/foot
              ^ mesa_foot/turn
              | mesa_foot/heard carried.from packet view now
      ; (mesa_wire/act reach turn.does)
      | _ turn.foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue
     > time=mesa_wire/tick
      = again (eq 0 (mod beat.inc mesa_foot/every))
        ;turn (? again (mesa_foot/tick time.now view foot) (mesa_foot/clock time.now view foot))
      ; (mesa_wire/act reach turn.does)
      | _ turn.foot view reach top beat.inc left key mute at ear queue
     > (./ack err)
      ? !(queue.len) (_ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue)
      = ;turn (mesa_foot/landed queue.at(0).request (mesa_wire/why err) foot)
        rest queue.drop(1)
      ; (mesa_wire/act reach turn.does)
      ; (mesa_wire/ask init.parent rest)
      ; (mesa_wire/look init.parent)
      | _ turn.foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear rest
    | _ foot view reach top beat left key mute at ear queue
  + here
    ^ path
    []
  + public
    ' This function gives the public key of a seed. It gives 0 when the bytes
    ' are not a seed.
    \ seed=nat
    ^ nat
    ? !(eq 33 (wid seed)) 0
    | Ed25519PublicKey seed
  + stopped
    ' This function tells if the record of the foot shows that the foot must
    ' stop. This is true when the record is gone or holds `stop`.
    \ binding=axal[saga]
    ^ bool
    ?> (./some version) binding.fil .true
    | gt (unpin version.myth).nat_at(['stop] 0) 0
  + wake
    ' This function connects to the port of this world, one time for each
    ' acceptor. This makes each acceptor stop its wait and ask the foot if it
    ' must continue.
    \ port=nat count=nat
    ^ nat
    = nothing=row[nat] []
    % left=nat count
    ^ nat
    ? (eq 0 port) 0
    ? !(left) 0
    ; (SendTo 0 "127.0.0.1" port (mesa_wire/frames 0 nothing))
    | _ left.dec
  + listen
    ' This function starts the acceptors that share the socket that the foot
    ' listens on.
    \ fd=nat count=nat
    ^ nat
    % left=nat count
    ^ nat
    ? !(left) 0
    = ;one (Spawn (mesa_wire/acceptor fd))
    ; (SendCaps one 0 [0])
    | _ left.dec
  + accepted
    ' This function tells if the supervisor accepted a write or a
    ' registration.
    \ reply=feet/task[unit]
    ^ bool
    ? reply
     > (./ack err)
      ? err
       > .none .true
       > (./some _) .false
    .false
  + submit
    ' This function sends one write and tells if the world accepted it.
    \ server=nat target=path change=writ
    ^ bool
    ; (SendCaps server (helm/card/step target change) [0])
    = ;[reply caps] (Recv[feet/task[unit]] 0)
    ; (helm/supervisor/close_caps caps)
    | mesa_wire/accepted reply
  + present
    ' If the world has no record of the foot, this function makes an empty
    ' one. It does not change a record that is there. The port, the key and
    ' all other data that the record holds stay the same. An empty poke makes
    ' no change. The world does not accept an empty poke in one case only:
    ' when there is no record. A make replaces the record.
    \ server=nat target=path
    ^ bool
    ? (mesa_wire/submit server target (writ/poke myth/new)) .true
    | mesa_wire/submit server target (writ/make myth/new)
  + seat
    ' This function gives two nodes their hands. It does this at each start.
    ' Thus the nodes have the hands of the code that runs.
    ' - The node of the foot, /<self>/io/mesa, gets the hand that does the
    '   errands of the foot (mesa_foot/form, net/seat).
    ' - /<self>/our gets the hand that tells the foot of news (net/herald,
    '   net/watch). The two names that this hand hears must hold a record:
    '   the kernel counts changes only below a name that holds one.
    \ server=nat self=iota
    ^ bool
    = home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
      ours=path [self 'our]
    ? !(mesa_wire/present server ours) .false
    ? !(mesa_wire/present server home.snoc('ask)) .false
    ? !(mesa_wire/submit server (net/hand self) (writ/make (form/card mesa_foot/form (lore/line "The hand of the Mesa foot")))) .false
    ? !(mesa_wire/submit server (net/horn self) (writ/make (form/card net/herald (lore/line "Tells the Mesa foot of news")))) .false
    ? !(mesa_wire/submit server home (writ/poke (net/seat self))) .false
    | mesa_wire/submit server ours (writ/poke (net/watch self))
  + start
    ' This function starts the foot of a world: it makes sure that the record
    ' /<self>/io/mesa is there and has its hand, and it registers the foot at
    ' that path. A world calls this function one time, when it starts. The
    ' mesa mount is the switch that makes a world call it.
    \ server=nat self=iota arguments=row[str]
    ^ nat
    = home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
    ? !(mesa_wire/present server home)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa foot record refused"))
    ? !(mesa_wire/seat server self)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa foot hand refused"))
    = registration | helm/supervisor/register home mesa_wire/run
    ; (SendCaps server registration [0])
    = ;[reply caps] | Recv[feet/task[unit]] 0
    ; | helm/supervisor/close_caps caps
    ? !(mesa_wire/accepted reply)
      (error (pails/t "Mesa foot registration failed"))
    0
